{"name":"Tyrian","hex":"#4e4d59","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Tyrian","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["From or relating to Tyre, Lebanon.","Having a purple colour produced by the dye Tyrian purple."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Person from Tyre."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Tyrian"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tyre, Lebanon","blocks":[{"text":"Tyre is a city in Lebanon, and one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world. It was one of the earliest Phoenician metropolises and the legendary birthplace of Europa, her brothers Cadmus and Phoenix, and Carthage's founder Dido (Elissa). The city has many ancient sites, including the Tyre Hippodrome, and was added as a whole to the list of UNESCO World Heritage Sites in 1984. The historian Ernest Renan described it as \"a city of ruins, built out of ruins\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/SubmergedEgyptianHarbour_TyreSour_Lebanon_RomanDeckert04112019.jpg/330px-SubmergedEgyptianHarbour_TyreSour_Lebanon_RomanDeckert04112019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyre%2C_Lebanon","wikidata":"Q82070"},{"title":"Tyrian (video game)","blocks":[{"text":"Tyrian is a vertically scrolling shooter developed by Eclipse Software for MS-DOS and published in 1995 by Epic MegaGames. Tyrian was programmed by Jason Emery, illustrated by Daniel Cook, and its music composed by Alexander Brandon and Andras Molnar. A slightly enhanced version was published in 1999 as Tyrian 2000. The game was re-released as freeware in 2004."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/73/Tryian_-_Box.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrian_(video_game)","wikidata":"Q1152304"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/ISS006-E-31938_TyreSurLebano